# Unknown future file types still need a deterministic default. Git detects
# whether the content is text; text uses LF and binary content stays untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Human-edited tunables. A config file whose bytes shift with the checkout would
# change the hash the run manifest records for it.
config/*.json text eol=lf

# The committed run state: the eval ledger, the fingerprints, the seen-URL
# store and the feed-health record. Rows are appended by CI on Linux and read by
# a developer on Windows; a CRLF rewrite would show every historical row as
# changed. `*.csv text eol=lf` near the bottom of this file is what pins them,
# so none of them needs a line of its own for that.
#
# Every one of those files carried `merge=union` until 2026-09-19, and that is
# what a lost push race used to stack. A job's checkout is pinned to the commit
# that triggered its run, so a second attempt could not see the rows the first
# attempt pushed, appended them again, and the union kept both - a key twice in
# a file every reader of it assumes holds each key once. Each writer now takes
# its own file inside the day directory, named for its run, attempt, job and
# shard, so there is nothing for a union to settle and nothing to sweep up
# after one.
#
# `state/feed-health/` is the one that deliberately takes NO union driver. Two
# plan jobs of one night each hold a verdict on every feed, and those two
# verdicts can disagree - so a union there would keep both and make the conflict
# quiet rather than remove it. The written-once name removes it.
#
# `state/seen/` is the one that never moved to a per-writer name and took the
# driver back instead. Its own line below says why the repeat it lets through
# changes no answer.

# The retirement record. One row is one address a server said was gone, keyed
# `(source_id, retired_on)` and true of that day whoever wrote it, so two runs
# that both met a dead address are not in disagreement and the union of both
# sides is the answer.
state/feed-retirements.csv text eol=lf merge=union

# One council shard's outcome. A row is one shard of one run and says nothing
# about another shard or another run, so two writers appending are independent
# and a repeat the union brings is dropped by `COUNCIL_SHARD_OUTCOME_KEY`.
state/llm-council/shard-outcomes/**/*.csv text eol=lf merge=union

# The four judge trees. A row is one measurement of one pair on one day, the
# reader keys on the pair and takes the newest, so two runs that both judged
# that day are not in disagreement and a repeat the union brings costs bytes
# and moves no verdict.
state/content-similarity-judge/metrics/**/*.csv text eol=lf merge=union
state/content-similarity-judge/merge-line-holdout-scores/**/*.csv text eol=lf merge=union
state/content-similarity-judge/scored-pairs/**/*.csv text eol=lf merge=union
state/content-similarity-judge/fitted-thresholds/**/*.csv text eol=lf merge=union

# The published day tree. It is one of the three collections that take a union
# driver, and it says so in its own words rather than inheriting one from a
# neighbour: a merge rule nobody chose is nobody's decision, and this one
# decides whether two runs of a day can both publish.
#
# `merge=union` is right here because a published row is independent of every
# other row - one item, one day, one address - so two runs of the same day that
# both append are not in disagreement, and the union of both sides is the
# answer. `ledger.load_published` keeps the earliest date per address, so a row
# the union brings twice costs bytes and never moves a publication date.
state/published/**/*.csv text eol=lf merge=union

# The cleanup record's day tree, the second collection that takes a union
# driver, and it names one for its own reason rather than the neighbour's.
#
# A row is one cleanup pass by one run - what it found, what it took, what the
# fuse held back - and no pass says anything about another, so two runs of the
# same day that both append are not in disagreement. A repeat the union brings
# costs bytes and moves no count: `ledger.append_visual_prunes` settles the day
# file against `VISUAL_PRUNE_KEY`, which is `(date, run_id)`, so a second row
# for one run is dropped and the first is kept.
state/visual-prunes/**/*.csv text eol=lf merge=union

# The first-sight record's day tree, the third and last collection that takes a
# union driver. It had none until 2026-09-22 and fell through to `*.csv text
# eol=lf` near the bottom of this file, so two runs of one day that both met a
# new address conflicted at the push over a file neither of them disagreed
# about.
#
# `merge=union` is right here for the reason written above `state/published/**`.
# A row says when one address was first met, `plan` appends a row only for an
# address `ledger.load_seen` did not already hold, and that reader keeps the
# EARLIEST stamp per address - so a row the union brings twice costs bytes and
# never moves an age. It is the one `state/` ledger that declares no key at all,
# and `ledger.keyed_paths` says so in its own words.
state/seen/**/*.csv text eol=lf merge=union

# The hand-marked holdout pairs. `merge=text` is what an unnamed path would get
# anyway, and it is written down because the reason is worth reading next to the
# union lines above and below. A person edits this file, so two edits of it are
# two people disagreeing about the same rows rather than two runs appending
# independent ones - and a union driver on a disagreement silently keeps both
# marks, which is a pair marked same-story and different-story at once. A text
# merge stops the push and asks, which is the right answer when the writer is a
# person.
state/content-similarity-judge/holdout-pairs.csv text eol=lf merge=text

# The training window. Deliberately NOT `merge=union`: it is a rolling window
# rather than an append-only ledger, so the union of two rolls is a file that
# holds evicted rows again and exceeds `finetune.corpus_rows`. The harvest
# records what its run saw, so a race is answered by replaying those rows onto
# the new base.
corpus/*.jsonl text eol=lf
corpus/*.json text eol=lf
corpus/*.txt text eol=lf

# Test fixtures are byte-exact inputs. A golden summary, a canary payload, or a
# captured page is compared against a fresh pipeline run, so any end-of-line
# conversion would fail the comparison on whichever machine has the other
# core.autocrlf setting.
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# Recorded process output, kept exactly as the process wrote it. `llama-server
# --help` pads its continuation lines, so the whitespace gate rejects the
# capture - and stripping those spaces would make the file a report of what the
# binary printed rather than the bytes it printed. A recording that has been
# tidied is no longer evidence, and evidence is the only reason to commit one.
tests/fixtures/runtime/*.txt -whitespace

# Source files are edited on Windows and Linux. Keep diffs about code, not line
# endings.
*.py text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.svelte text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Prose is reviewed line by line. A checkout must not rewrite the document.
*.md text eol=lf

# The prompts are fingerprint inputs: `fingerprint.build_inputs` hashes the
# prompt string, so their bytes are part of the determinism stamp. Python's
# `read_text` folds CRLF to LF before that hash, which is why a CRLF copy has
# never broken a fingerprint - but an unpinned file still shows as modified on
# a fresh Linux checkout, and a dirty tree is what aborts the publish push.
*.txt text eol=lf

# The rest of the authored surface. Every one of these was unpinned until a
# CRLF blob in a single doc aborted a rebase and discarded a whole day's
# digest; pinning the file type is cheaper than finding the next one.
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.ipynb text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
.editorconfig text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Shell a workflow step runs. A CRLF blob here is not a diff nuisance, it is a
# broken job: the runner's bash reads the carriage return as part of the shebang
# and as part of every command.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Published digest payloads. Their sha256 is recorded in the run manifest and
# re-verified, so the stored bytes and the checked-out bytes must agree.
frontend/public/**/*.json text eol=lf
frontend/public/**/*.md text eol=lf

# The month search index's vector sibling: raw int8 laid end to end, not text.
# An end-of-line conversion here would rewrite bytes inside a vector, and every
# offset after it, and each one would still decode without complaint.
frontend/public/assist/index/*.bin binary

# Downloaded, not authored: never diff or eol-convert these.
*.woff2 binary
*.gguf binary
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.onnx binary
*.wasm binary
